  "articleBody": "Hiring someone to renovate your kitchen is a major commitment, and signing the wrong contract can quickly lead to budget creep or mid-project delays. Before committing your hard-earned money, you need clarity on exact scopes, sub-trade coordination, and allowances. A trustworthy contractor should be clear about who manages plumbing and electrical trade specialists on-site.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start\n- Request itemized scopes that identify materials, fixtures, and appliances by specific manufacturer model numbers rather than generic descriptions.\n- Confirm proof of active general liability and workers' compensation coverage before demolition starts.\n- Ask how change orders are priced, documented, and approved if hidden rot or old plumbing problems appear inside your walls.\n- Verify whether the bid includes necessary permits and required local municipal inspections.\n- Maintain a dedicated 10% to 15% contingency reserve in your personal budget for unexpected underlying wall or subfloor conditions.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nTaking down load-bearing partitions, relocating drain lines, or adding heavy appliance circuits demands experienced trade management. For homeowners in the Denver and Aurora area looking for hands-on project management, reach out to Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC by calling 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
